<div class="article-body">

<p>A 1020 microgreen tray gives you between 8 and 27 ounces of harvest, and the crop you pick is almost the whole story. I measured this myself on my own shelves: a tray of broccoli comes in around 10 ounces, while sunflower shoots from the same-size tray hit 27. The difference is not luck. It is two things you control, and this article tells you both with real numbers.</p>

<p>Here's the short version: <strong>big-seeded crops (sunflower, peas) give you 20 to 27 ounces per tray. Leafy brassicas (broccoli, kale, cabbage) give you 8 to 10 ounces per tray.</strong> If you have been comparing your broccoli tray to someone's sunflower photos and feeling bad, that is the reason. You are not growing it wrong.</p>

<p>Full disclosure: I am not a farmer. I am a guy with four trays on a wire shelf who killed plenty of batches and then started weighing every harvest so I could answer the exact question you are asking. I weigh every tray now. These numbers are real, checked against what commercial growers report, not pulled from a marketing page.</p>

<h2 id="the-number">1. A 1020 Tray Gives You 8 to 27 Ounces</h2>

<p>Here is the key insight: nobody can give you one flat "microgreen yield per tray" number, because the range is huge and it comes from plant biology, not your skill. Sunflower shoots are dense and heavy. Broccoli microgreens are light and airy. Same tray, wildly different weights.</p>

<p>Pay attention to this number: <strong>the 1020 tray is the standard, 10 by 20 inches.</strong> That is the farming unit everyone means when they say "per tray." If you grow in smaller containers, divide down from this. An 8 ounce harvest means a full tray you would actually serve from, not a snack cup.</p>

<p>My own measurements over the last year, tray per tray:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Broccoli: about 10 ounces.</li>
  <li>Kale and cabbage: about 9 to 10 ounces.</li>
  <li>Mustard: a touch under the brassicas, around 8 to 9 ounces.</li>
  <li>Pea shoots: 20 ounces.</li>
  <li>Sunflower shoots: 27 ounces.</li>
</ul>

<p>Commercial growers report the same spread. This is not my specific grumpy shelf. It is the actual nature of these crops.</p>

<h2 id="by-crop">2. Yield by Crop, With the Why</h2>

<p>Why do sunflower and pea shoots blow past broccoli by 2 to 3 times? The seed carries the answer. A sunflower seed is huge and full of stored energy. A broccoli seed is a speck. That stored energy becomes stem, and stem is weight. Bigger seed, heavier plant, denser tray.</p>

<p>Here is why that matters: if your goal is maximum food from minimum effort, you want big-seeded crops. One tray of sunflower shoots at 27 ounces beats three trays of broccoli at 30 total ounces, for a fraction of the tray cost. My weekly rotation is mostly peas and sunflowers for exactly this math.</p>

<p>But weight is not the only score. Broccoli and kale have more concentrated nutrition per ounce, and they taste milder. A 10 ounce broccoli tray still fills a lot of salads. Pick density and volume for salads and stir fries, or pick the light greens for snacking and garnishing.</p>

<p>This is where it gets interesting: the heaviest tray is not always the most valuable, and the lightest is not a failure. Weight is one number in the ledger. Taste, nutrition, and how fast it grows all count too.</p>

<h2 id="what-decides">3. What Actually Decides Your Harvest Weight</h2>

<p>Three things set your yield, and only one of them is the crop. The other two are mistakes people make for months before realizing it.</p>

<p>First, seed density. This is the big one. Too few seeds and you grow a bare, patchy tray that weighs half what it should. The fix is a proper seeding rate, not a sprinkle. You want seeds close enough that nearly every square inch has a stem. I cover this in the next section because it is worth its own heading.</p>

<p>Second, harvest timing. Cut the tray a couple days late and you lose weight to stem that turns into tough pith, plus you eat daring bites. Cut early and you rob yourself of volume. Harvest in that day to two-day window and you get the full weight without the chew.</p>

<p>Third, growing conditions: light, water, and airflow. This is the section most guides bury, so here is the plain version. A tray grown under weak light leans and stretches, and a stretched stem is watery and light. Give it real light. Keep the medium damp, never soggy. And keep air moving so you do not get mold, which kills whole patches and quietly cuts your weight by a quarter.</p>

<h2 id="density">4. The Seeding Density Cheat Code</h2>

<p>I thought I had it all figured out. Then I planted broccoli "by eye" for three trays in a row and weighed them at 5 to 6 ounces each, half of what they should have been. The tray looked okay to the eye. The scale told the truth. The fix was not harder work. It was a measured seeding rate.</p>

<p>Here is the honest truth about seeding microgreens: your eyes lie to you. A scattered handful looks full until harvest, when you see all the empty mat you did not notice before. Weight is unforgiving. The only thing that fixes it is a density you measure, not guess.</p>

<p>The range that works, per 1020 tray: 25 to 30 grams of small brassica seed, 70 grams of peas, and 100 to 110 grams of sunflower seed. For the heavy crops, weigh it once and then you will know exactly what a properly loaded tray looks like. Your eye recalibrates fast.</p>

<p>Here is why that matters: seeding density is the single cheapest fix in the whole hobby. It costs nothing, takes one minute with a scale, and it can double your harvest from the exact same tray, medium, and light. Most people chase expensive lights and fancy racks before they fix the one thing that gives them back ounces for free.</p>

<p>Here is the exact gear I use for full-weight trays:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=1020+microgreen+seed+tray+without+holes&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">1020 microgreen tray, no holes</a>, about $8 for a 2-pack. No holes means the medium holds moisture and the roots stay happy.</li>
  <li>Weighed <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=microgreen+seeds+various+crops&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">seeds for your crop</a>. Brassica mixes are cheap and fast, and packets of sunflower and pea seed give you the heavy trays.</li>
  <li>A cheap <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=kitchen+scale+grams&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">kitchen scale in grams</a>, about $10. This is the tool that killed my guessing. Weigh your seed once per crop and write the number on the bag.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="honest">5. Why Your Tray Might Come In Light (And the Honest Fix)</h2>

<p>Three things to check if your last tray was under 8 ounces, in order of how often they are the real cause.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Your seed density.</strong> This is number one by a mile. If you planted by handful, this is probably it. Weigh your seed for the next tray and watch the number jump.</li>
  <li><strong>Weak light.</strong> A tall, pale, stretched tray is under-lit. The stems are hollow and watery. Move the light closer (check the <a href="/posts/grow-light-distance-chart" >grow light distance chart</a>) and weigh it again.</li>
  <li><strong>Mold or rot.</strong> If patches discolored or the mat smells, you lost weight to rot. That is airflow and moisture, not planting.</li>
</ul>

<p>One more honest thing: even a perfect tray has a range, not a fixed number. Temperature, the specific seed lot, even how long you soaked the big seeds, all nudge the final weight by an ounce or two. Do not chase the top number every single tray. Chase the range for your crop, then get consistently inside it.</p>

<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> One 1020 tray of microgreens gives you 8 to 10 ounces of leafy brassicas, or 20 to 27 ounces of pea and sunflower shoots. The crop picks the weight, and seeding density is the fix that doubles it. Measure your seed, harvest on time, and give the tray real light. Start with a good <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=1020+microgreen+seed+tray+without+holes&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">1020 tray</a>, weigh out the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=microgreen+seeds+various+crops&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">seed</a> with a cheap <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=kitchen+scale+grams&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">kitchen scale</a>, and you will be harvesting full-weight trays in a week.</p>

